// ============================================================================
// XENHARMONIC DAILY — 17edo exploration
// Saturday slot: a short, single-continuous pattern exploring a tuning
// outside standard 12edo, via Strudel's xen() function (see learn/xen.md).
// ============================================================================
//
// THEORY NOTES:
// 17edo divides the octave into 17 EQUAL steps, each 1200/17 ≈ 70.59 cents
// (vs. 100 cents per semitone in familiar 12edo). Its best fifth is 10 steps
// ≈ 705.9 cents — noticeably WIDER than 12edo's 700-cent fifth, putting it
// in the "superpythagorean" temperament family. That wide fifth pushes the
// tuning's thirds toward a "neutral" color, sitting between a 12edo major
// and minor third rather than landing on either — a flavor 12-tone tuning
// literally cannot produce.
//
// xen("17edo") maps a pattern of integer scale-STEPS (not scale degrees in
// a named mode — raw 1/17-octave steps) straight to frequency, assuming a
// base of 220Hz (A3) at step 0 and repeating every octave (2/1), per
// learn/xen.md. Unlike tune(), xen() bakes the freq conversion in — no
// separate .mul()/.freq() needed, mirroring the docs' own examples
// (`i("0 8 18").xen("31edo").piano()`).
//
// Because everything is relative to step-0 = 220Hz, octave register is
// controlled by adding/subtracting whole multiples of 17 (one octave =
// 17 steps) to the step numbers — not by .mul()-ing the output, which
// would double-apply the frequency mapping xen() already did.
//
// RHYTHM: melody uses .euclid(7, 17) — 7 onsets distributed across 17
// steps per cycle. The step COUNT (17) is the same number as the EDO's
// own division count, so the meter and the tuning share one number,
// the same "tradition's own math drives the pattern" idea used for
// Carnatic melakarta days, applied here to a tuning system instead of a
// raga system.
//
// gm_kalimba below follows Strudel's General MIDI soundfont naming
// convention (same family as gm_pad_warm, confirmed elsewhere in this
// repo) but wasn't individually reconfirmed against the live sample list
// offline — if it doesn't resolve, swap in whatever plucked/pitched-
// percussion sound is closest in the REPL's "sounds" tab; this is a taste
// choice, not a structural one.
// ============================================================================

setcpm(48) // 16 cycles @ 48cpm = 20s total (see CYCLES below / daily/2026-08-22.json)
let CYCLES = 16

// low drone: root (step 0) and the 17edo "fifth" (step 10), two octaves
// down (-34 steps = -2 octaves), alternating slowly across 4 cycles
let drone = i("<-34 -24>/4")
  .xen("17edo")
  .sound("gm_pad_warm")
  .gain(.25)
  .room(4)
  .attack(2).release(3)

// melody: wanders fairly evenly across the 17 available steps (0, 3, 6,
// 8, 10, 13, 15 -- roughly a 7-note spread through the octave) so the ear
// hears the tuning's overall color, not just notes near the root.
// euclid(7,17) ties the onset count + step count to the EDO itself.
let melody = i("0 3 6 8 10 13 15 10 8 6 3 0")
  .euclid(7, 17)
  .xen("17edo")
  .sound("gm_kalimba")
  .gain(.5)
  .room(2)
  .delay(.15)
  .sometimes(x => x.ply(2)) // <- improv hook: occasional double-strike

// acid-flavored bass (the taste layer from CREATIVE-DIRECTION.md applies
// even on a math/tuning-driven day) -- one octave down (-17 steps),
// walking between root and fifth
let bass = i("-17 -7 -17 -24")
  .xen("17edo")
  .sound("sawtooth")
  .lpf(sine.range(200, 1400).slow(6))
  .lpq(14)
  .distort(.25)
  .gain(.35)

let pulse = s("hh*8")
  .degradeBy(.4)
  .gain(.12)

stack(drone, melody, bass, pulse)
